ing to be  out by now  so uh we could kind of get a little bit  more into the meat and potatoes of what  the program will look like  but uh we are being told that they're at  the last stage  of approval so we're hoping they'll be  out within the next couple weeks here  which will kind of give a clearer  picture of everything that  will be in the program um i also want to  highlight  that uh there's a public comment period  associated with those regulations  so um you know you guys are obviously  you know involved in the industry  studying the industry experts and hemp  and and uh what's kind of the cutting  edge movement now  and we definitely encourage encourage  you guys to submit comments  because uh you know we're not gonna get  everything right the the first time  and uh viewing this more as a  partnership  will definitely help us help us make the  best you know cannabinoid hemp program  we can um and i think we're up to now  what 16 different states  that have their own unique kind of uh  cannabinoid hemp or cbd regulations out  there  so it really is a rapidly developing  regulatory framework right now there's a  patchwork of different states  and we borrowed a little bit from others  we borrowed a little bit from the  dietary supplement industry from the  food regulations  and uh we're hoping hoping just to make  something that works  and is flexible so  getting started uh today just want to  talk a little bit about the  legislative background kind of the  history of how how the bill came to pass  and um why it was kind of placed at the  at the department of health  medical marijuana program and we'll go  over the program objectives  for what we're trying to accomplish here  a brief description of the different  license types between processors and  retailers  um look at the product standards that  we're trying to trying to hold these two  and  really those are really building off of  the great work that ag  markets has already done and then a  quick timeline  and next steps so we can kind of  telegraph what the next three to six  months will look like  so as many of you are probably aware a  bill passed in in early 2020  establishing  a hemp program in new york state and as  uh chris just just mentioned in the last  uh segment there  uh the usda will actually be the uh  regulating cultivation  for the 2021 uh grow season due to kind  of  the cumbersome requirements with the  interim final rule  and then the processing and retailing of  cannabinoid hemp  will be going over to the department of  health  now uh it was really placed there to  kind of build off the expertise  that had been developed from regulating  the medical marijuana program  there's a lot of synergies in terms of  how the plant is extracted  packaging labeling requirements  manufacturing requirements  testing so we figured it would be a good  place to kind of  merge and you know always thinking about  the taxpayer uh sharing some resources  here  so we can can um  get a get a good head start and i want  to  uh highlight this first sub bullet here  about the transition from a research  program to a traditional license program  because it it is really significant um  when again markets first developed their  program back in 2015  it was really you know focused on what  we heard today in research  and then all of a sudden cbd exploded  and everyone wanted to grow it everyone  wanted to process it and it became much  more about the  the marketability in the  commercialization aspects  of of hemp which which is great but  under a research program you're kind of  limited  in in the sort of uh relationship  between the regulator  and the licensee so so uh under this new  framework we're hoping that we can  really uh  grow and help develop the hemp industry  in new york state  so what are we talking about uh  cannabinoid hemp um  hemp is defined as any product that is  used for its cannabinoid content for  human consumption or topical application  so hemp see