ontains  cannabinoids they're not getting ripped  off and and they have you know the  appropriate warnings or disclosures of  what's in the bottle  and kind of tied into that is is this  last point about education and  enforcement  so we we definitely understand that you  know you can have the best rules in the  world  but if people aren't following them or  they're not being uh  enforced it doesn't really matter so uh  education  enforcement are going to be a really key  pillar of the program and  um we're small now but we're hoping that  we can ramp up  in terms of resources over the next few  years and dedicate a lot of a lot of  a lot of those resources to that effort  so a quick overview of the license types  in the new program  we split the processor into two  different licenses so  there's the extraction and manufacturing  license  and then the manufacturing only license  uh the the key difference here  is really um the extractor license  because  you know you're using different  equipment and different technologies  and there's a different level of risk  for extraction that that is going to be  its own  kind of a license type assuming that not  every business will want to do that  and then the manufacturing only license  will be businesses who are purchasing  kind of that intermediary product that's  already been extracted so your isolates  distillates  whole plant extracts and then  manufacturing a product from there  so really just the idea there was just  allowing different  opportunities for businesses to to enter  enter the supply chain  uh based on their business model  and the last uh license here is the hemp  retailer license  um our idea here is really to make it  more of a permit or a registration  this is just a way for the department to  kind of get some insight into where  these products are being sold  um and then also gets our foot in the  door so we can start educating these  retailers about you know how to source  a quality cannabinoid hemp 